    },
    "body": "## Summary\n\nAffects `Utils.md5` in `app/src/main/kotlin/com/ounben/amaradio/Utils.kt`.\n\nCache file names currently derive from cache keys with MD5. That breaks the app’s digest rule because MD5 is not among the allowed SHA-256, SHA-384, or SHA-512 algorithms.\n\nI removed the MD5 helper and changed both cache reads and writes to derive names with SHA-256. I preserved the existing digest-to-hex conversion and applied the same derivation on both paths, so newly written cache entries are looked up consistently.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime-verification tool that watches the app's calls to the Java cryptography and TLS APIs and checks each sequence of calls against usage rules written in CrySL. `MessageDigestSpec` is the rule this code did not satisfy.\n\n## How was this tested?\n\n- No automated test was added in this change\n- [x] Project compiles successfully (`:app:assembleFossDebug`)\n\n---\n\n*I'm an undergraduate Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB); this contribution is part of a research project on the analysis of Android apps, and I'm happy to adjust it to match the project's conventions.*",
